\section{Threat model and assumption}
\subsection{Adversary goals}
During memory extraction attacks, attackers primarily look for the secret built at $S=f(0)$. By creating a core dump that takes a snapshot of the processes' RAM using gcore, an attacker can read out the secret, $S$, in plain text. However, if an alternate layer of security is added, the secret, $S$, may not be visible directly within the ram. Instead, cryptographic systems depending on an algorithm such as SSS on centralized process memory can provide the attacker with all $k$ distinct shares to reconstruct the secret, $S=f(0)$ using Lagrange interpolation, whereas an attacker attaining $k-1$ shares leads to a deficiency in total shares $k$ required to reconstruct the secret. An attacker utilizing memory extraction executes scripts under the same context as the processes' UID for direct access, bypassing root privileges for read access on this file \texttt{/proc/\$PID/mem} where PID is the process identification number.
\subsection{Assumptions}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Kernel Security: } Assuming the device holding $k$ shares across multiple processes is not infected with a Ring 0 rootkit, IPC data transfer remains under control. As a Ring 0 rootkit gives direct access over the kernel, leaving the system highly compromised and vulnerable. Systems exposed to Ring 0 rootkit malware lose control over programs that facilitate data transfer such as IPC, meaning the architecture fails in such a situation.
    \item \textbf{Core dumps: } Although zeroization and memory wiping are integral processes in clearing the secret, $S$, the secret is still exposed during a minimal time frame as plain text sitting in central process memory. Prior to zeroization, an attacker utilizing asynchronous core / memory dumps may intercept $S$.
    \item \textbf{Hardware attacks: } Direct hardware and physical attacks are out of scope when evaluating the proposed solution. Direct attacks are not accounted for as the solution fragments $k$ shares across multiple processes, accounting mainly for attempted mathematical and software compromise. However, hardware attacks such as cold boot attacks extract data from ram without requiring root privilege or core dumps, utilizing only tailgating or social engineering to execute such an attack which the proposed solution does not cover.
\end{itemize}

\section{Implementation of proposed solution}
\subsection{Architecture}
Effectively utilizing SSS and maximizing security requires fragmenting key shares $n$ across multiple processes which prevents runtime memory extraction. Key fragmentation is accomplished by communication between different processes through IPC (Interprocess Communication) pipes. Using pipes is considered advantageous in terms of speed prioritization and minimal memory allocation by utilizing uni-directional data flow, decreasing risks associated with data coupling which prove it to be of a better mechanism for cryptographic systems implementing key fragmentation. After generating $n$ distinct $x,y$ shares, data is transmitted from one process to another through IPC pipes ensuring each process gets its own share with no data coupling, granting cross-process security for other non compromised processes.

\subsection{Finite fields implementation}
For $\mathbb{Z}_p$ to effectively reduce computing errors and prevent floating point errors, $p$ must be a number strictly greater than the secret, $S$, and must be a prime number. Created through cryptographically secure pseudorandom number generators, CSPRNGs, with a range of $[0,p-1]$, $a_j$ mitigates risk of predictability by relying on common entropy sources such as \texttt{/dev/urandom}. Unlike using CSPRNGs, default random generation libraries default to predictable seeds and can cause the attacker to predict all generated coefficients and calculate for the secret $S$, without needing $k$ shares. Additionally, all random coefficients, $a_j$ must be less than $p$, and greater than or equal to 0, $0 \le a_j < p$. During evaluation of $x_i$ shares, the polynomial equation simplifies to $y_i = f(x_i) \pmod p$. Implementing $\mathbb{Z}_p$ is crucial in ensuring boundaries are placed and no algorithm can predict the slope from a given set of points. Crucially, after generating random coefficients through CSPRNGS, zeroization plays an integral role in protecting $S$ by changing all $a_j$ coefficients into zeros, and must be done within a correct timeframe to mitigate risks of asynchronous memory dumps.

\subsection{Interprocess Communication (IPC)}
Implementation of IPC pipes in the context of independent sharing requires unidirectional flow and exclusive context management to prevent accidental secret extraction which can be accidentally done through \texttt{fork()}. Using \texttt{fork()} increases the risk of secret extraction by allowing worker processes $W_i$, where i is the index of each worker process, to inherit from the orchestrator, resulting in information coupling. The orchestrator sends $k$ shares to each $W_i$ worker process using the \texttt{write end} of the file descriptor, \texttt{FD} where FD is an integer that IPC utilizes for a description of open channels. \texttt{FD[0]} is universally known as the read handler in most Linux/POSIX systems which $W_i$ use to read incoming data from the orchestrator. Whereas \texttt{FD[1]} is the write handler used to send data to other processes, utilized by the orchestrator. In the context of independent sharing, the use of \texttt{fork()} is tenuous. Effectively mitigating accidental worker inheritance from \texttt{fork()} requires the closure of reading channels \texttt{FD[0]} to prevent accidental reading of data from the worker, before individual usage of \texttt{FD[1]}, write handle, of the orchestrator to each individual worker processes $W_i$. Using \texttt{close(FD[0])}, the orchestrator closes each read channel before sending $(x_i,f(x_i))$ shares to $W_i$ workers. In the context of the worker, $W_i$, the worker closes the writing channel on \texttt{FD[1]} using \texttt{close(FD[1])} before the orchestrator sends individual shares $(x_i,f(x_i))$ to prevent accidentally sending information to the worker. Additionally, the worker $W_i$ closes the writing and reading handle of neighboring workers $W_j$ using \texttt{close(FD[1])} and \texttt{close(FD[0])} requiring a \texttt{for} loop for each $W_j$ worker instance. Substantially mitigating the risk of the worker, $W_i$, reading other inherited shares of other workers.

\subsection{Hardened zeroization}
During zeroization, standard functions to eliminate variables from ram such as \texttt{memset()} or simply setting pointers to \texttt{NULL} may be of lower priority to low level languages that prioritize speed and computational efficiency. After completion of IPC pipe transmission and using \texttt{memset()}, compilers often remove the function entirely. Known as Dead-Store elimination, it often occurs when the compiler acknowledges that the coefficients $a_j$ are never read again during the remainder of the program and instead of calling \texttt{memset()} the compiler ignores the function and leaves variables in ram in an act of prioritizing speed. To mitigate such risks, the proposed solution prioritizes hardened memory wiping, \texttt{explicit\_bzero()}, as a direct substitution for \texttt{memset()}. \texttt{explicit\_bzero()} ensures zeroization regardless of the compiler's speed optimization by halting the compiler's ability to ignore calls to \texttt{explicit\_bzero()}.

\section{Experimental results}
\subsection{Testing Environment}
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Hardware: } x86\_64 with an 8 core cpu and 16 gb of ram.
    \item \textbf{Operating system \& kernel: } POSIX compliant linux environment running linux kernel 6.x utilizing system calls: \texttt{fork(2)}, \texttt{sigaction(2)}, \texttt{pipe(2)}.
    \item \textbf{Compiler requirements: } Programmed in C, and compiled using GCC under 14.x with \texttt{-02} (standard) and \texttt{-03} (aggressive) optimization flags to juxtapose and test Dead-Store elimination behaviour.
    \item \textbf{System Libraries: } Linked to standard \texttt{glibc} providing \texttt{explicit\_bzero()} for hardened zeroization
    \item \textbf{Experiment parameters: } Share counts were shared across $n \in \{5,10,25,50,100\}$ with the reconstruction threshold set to $k = \lceil n/2 \rceil$.
    \item \textbf{Finite field parameters: } Polynomials should be calculated over finite field $\mathbb{Z}_p$ using a 32 bit prime modulus $p=2^{31}-1$.
    \item \textbf{Size and timing: } Each parameter combination is ran $n=1000$ independent trials iteration. Execution time was recorded via \texttt{CLOCK\_MONOTONIC} to calculate mean latency and standard deviation. 
\end{itemize} 

